# Authoring eseq instruments and effects

You are working in the eseq user folder. When the user asks for a new
instrument ("make me a poly synth with a wavefolder") or an audio effect ("a
tape delay with wow and flutter"), you write two small source files into a new
folder here. eseq compiles them to native code and shows them in its browser.
When the user asks to change one, edit its files in place.

## Where things are

| What | Path |
| --- | --- |
| Your instruments (write here) | `{{USER_INSTRUMENTS}}` |
| Your effects (write here) | `{{USER_EFFECTS}}` |
| Instrument rules (read first) | `{{AUTHORING}}/instrument-reference.md` |
| Effect rules (read first) | `{{AUTHORING}}/effect-reference.md` |
| DGenLisp language reference | `{{DGENLISP_REFERENCE}}/DGenLispReadme.md` |
| DGenLisp operator catalog (large JSON; search it) | `{{DGENLISP_REFERENCE}}/dgenlisp-operators.json` |
| Factory instruments (read-only examples) | `{{FACTORY_INSTRUMENTS}}` |
| Factory effects (read-only examples) | `{{FACTORY_EFFECTS}}` |
| The `eseq` command-line tool | `{{ESEQ}}` |

The factory folders are read-only examples. Never edit them. Don't touch the
user's projects, samples, recordings or other folders here unless they ask.

## What an instrument or effect is

One folder containing:

- `dsp.lisp`: the signal processing, written in DGenLisp. An instrument turns
  gate/pitch/velocity inputs into audio; one voice is compiled per note. An
  effect processes stereo audio in to stereo audio out.
- `ui.lisp`: the panel. It holds exactly one `(defsynth-ui ...)` form for an
  instrument, or `(defeffect-ui ...)` for an effect, built from the
  `eseq.effects.custom-ui-lego` helpers and naming params from `dsp.lisp`.

The folder name is what the user sees in the browser, so use a short
human-readable name such as `Fold Poly` or `Tape Echo`. Sub-folders become folders in the browser
(`{{USER_INSTRUMENTS}}/Drums/Tight Hat/`, `{{USER_INSTRUMENTS}}/Bass/Acid Mono/`);
use one when the user already has a matching folder. Before creating a folder,
check the name isn't already taken.

## Workflow

1. Read the rules file for what you are making (`instrument-reference.md` or
   `effect-reference.md`). They are short, strict and specific to eseq. Look up
   operators in the DGenLisp reference as you need them.
2. Study one or two factory examples close to the request. Read their
   `dsp.lisp` for DSP technique. Good small starting points:
   - synths: `{{FACTORY_INSTRUMENTS}}/Synths/Revsynt/dsp.lisp`,
     `{{FACTORY_INSTRUMENTS}}/Synths/Vox/dsp.lisp`
   - drums: `{{FACTORY_INSTRUMENTS}}/Drums/Boom Bap Kick/dsp.lisp` (for a
     simple drum, the one-shot recipe in the instrument rules is a better
     start)
   - physical models: `{{FACTORY_INSTRUMENTS}}/Physical Models/PM Flute/dsp.lisp`
   - effects with standard panels: `{{FACTORY_EFFECTS}}/stereo-tremolo/`,
     `{{FACTORY_EFFECTS}}/lexilush/`

   Factory instrument panels use bespoke surface modules. For your own panels,
   use the lego helpers shown in the rules files instead. Some older factory
   sources break the rules files' conventions; when they disagree, follow the
   rules files. Their "Recipes" section covers one-shot drum envelopes,
   feedback delays (`delay` takes samples), LFOs and history cells.
3. Write `dsp.lisp` and `ui.lisp` into the new folder. For the panel, choose
   the standard or dense lego layout by how many controls you will show, not
   by how many params the DSP declares; start simple and only go dense when a
   standard layout would not fit.
4. Check your work, and repeat until it passes:

   ```sh
   "{{ESEQ}}" instrument check "{{USER_INSTRUMENTS}}/<Name>"
   "{{ESEQ}}" effect check "{{USER_EFFECTS}}/<Name>"
   ```

   The check compiles the DSP, validates the panel, renders a test note or
   test signal (it fails on silence or clipping), and draws the panel with
   eseq's own renderer. `FAIL` lines give the stage and the exact error; fix
   that and run it again. `warn` lines are conventions worth following but not
   blockers. If the panel render fails with "eseq's own UI failed to load; not
   caused by these files", the problem is in the installation, not your
   folder: don't try to fix it, tell the user, and use `--no-render` to check
   everything else. On success it prints the path of a PNG of the panel (rewritten on each run). **Open that
   image and look at it**: check for overlapping or clipped controls, empty
   space, and red `missing: <param>` labels, then adjust the layout.
5. Tell the user it's ready and how to find it. eseq picks up new folders
   while it runs: the instrument appears in the browser's **Instruments** tab
   under **Library** (effects: **Audio FX** tab, **Custom**). Summarize the
   controls and suggest settings to try.

Making sound is the job: a patch that passes the check but sounds thin or
harsh isn't done. Pick sensible defaults (moderate levels, a musical filter
setting, short attack) so the first note the user plays sounds good.

## Changing an existing instrument

Edit its `dsp.lisp`/`ui.lisp` and run the check again. A `ui.lisp` change
shows up in eseq by itself. A `dsp.lisp` change to something already on a
track needs one gesture from the user, so tell them: select the track and
choose **Edit > Reload Instrument From Disk** (for an effect, click its header
and choose **Edit > Reload Selected Effect From Disk**). Knob values are kept
for params whose names didn't change. Keep existing param
names where you can: projects store knob values and step locks by param name,
and a renamed param loses them.

## Sharing

The user can share their work as a package: **File > Export Package…** in
eseq, or

```sh
"{{ESEQ}}" package export <author>/<name> 0.1.0 <out-dir> instrument:<Folder> effect:<Folder>
```

`<Folder>` is the path under the instruments or effects folder above.
